 ## 🧠 How it works
 
-1. While FLM reads a prompt it saves a **snapshot** of the model's state at the end of the system prompt and tools, and another one at the end of the prompt.
+1. While FLM reads a prompt it saves a **snapshot** of the model's state at the end of the system prompt and tools (when they are at least 512 tokens long), and another one at the end of the prompt. When the prompt before shared everything up to the last user turn (a user message, or tool results) and went on with another one, it also saves one at the start of that turn. This snapshot serves prompts which share everything but their last message, like the same few-shot examples or the same document followed by different questions. A chat, whose every turn only continues the turn before, does not save it, since saving it needs a prefill pass of its own. It is saved only when it is at least 512 tokens into the prompt and adds at least `--prefix-min-gain` tokens to the system prompt snapshot and to what the prompt resumed from. FLM compares a prompt only with the prompt just before it, so two prompts with the same history have to come one after the other: with a request in between (a title call, another conversation), or with two sets of examples used in turn, it is not saved.
 2. For a new request, FLM looks for the **longest saved snapshot whose text is exactly the beginning of the new prompt**. It restores that snapshot and reads only the rest.
 3. Snapshots are matched on the exact tokens, so two different conversations that start with the same system prompt reuse the same snapshot of it. Images are matched by their content: the same picture is reused, a different picture is read again (the text before it is still reused).
 4. A short request with its own system prompt (for example the title call some agents send after each turn) does not erase the snapshots of the main conversation.
